
<html>
<head><title> Kravchuk:Introduction Twistons </title></head>
<body>
<p>
<H2> Twistons </H2>
 
This example, provides some heuristics
 rather then really proposes a ``physical theory.''
Treat it as a ``toy theory'' that describes hypothetical particles called``twistons.''  Krawtchouk polynomials may be treated as generalized potentials
of interaction.
 
<p>
A twiston may have one of two states: regular or twisted. This can be
viewed as two possible states of a piece of a ribbon band (representing
a twiston):
\begin{figure}[htp]     % FIGURE
\caption{M\" obius band.}
\end{figure}
<p>
As well, we may associate ``spin up'' and ``spin down'' respectively as an
... aid, but notice the difference: spin is interchangeable with respect
to viewer position, twist is not.
<p>
Now, let a system be composed of n such particles.  One may calculate
the total twist of a system; this will be just the number j of twisted
particles among the n particles.
<p>
The system state may be thus described by two numbers \{i,j\} <p>
Define the ``1-st potential'' of a
system of n twistons with j twistons twisted as
<dl>
<dt>1.<dd> k<sup> (n) </sup>_1(j) =j
 
or in terms of x:
 
<dt>1'. <dd> K<sup> (n) </sup>_1(x)={1 \over 2} (n-x)
 
Next question is the potential coming from pairwise interactions. Two
particles interact by opening themselves and forming a new particle, as
these two example show:
<p>
      
<p>
Gluing two twisted particles cancels the twist.  In other sense, the
potential of interaction of a pair is the product of the sum of two
twists modulo 2.  Define the total pairwise interaction potential
(energy) of a system of n twistons with j particles twisted to be
the sum of such potentials over all pairs in the system:
 
<dt>2.<dd> k<sup> (n) </sup>_2(j) = {j \choose 2} - {j \choose 1}\cdot{i \choose 1}
                  + {i \choose 2}
              = jjjj
 
where i was understood in terms of i=n-j; or in terms of x:
<p>
 
<dt>2'.<dd> K<sup> (n) </sup>_2(x) = xxxx
 
<p>
Hypothesizing, but still in the spirit of modern physics, one may want to
add also higher order interactions.  Thus, every triple of the system,
when interacting, forms a ribbon that is twisted as a sum of twists
modulo 2.  See the example:
<p>
      
<p>
This leads two the following total energy of 3-degree interaction,
depending on j or x:
<p>
 
<dt>3.<dd> k<sup> (n) </sup>_3 (j) = {j \choose 3} - {j \choose 2}\cdot{i \choose 1}
                  +{j \choose 2}\cdot {i \choose 2} - {i\choose 3}
              = jjjj
 
or in terms of x:
<p>
 
<dt>3'.<dd> K<sup> (n) </sup>_3(x) = xxxx
 
Notice that the values of these polynomials coincide exactly with the
entries of the corresponding Krawtchouk matrix K<sup> (3) </sup>.  In general, one
may define the p-th Krawtchouk polynomial of order n as
<p>
K<sup> (n) </sup>_p(j) = \sum <sub> k=0 </sub><sup> p </sup> \ {j \choose k}{{n-j}\choose {p-k}}
<p>
Interestingly, in terms of variable x, Krawtchouk polynomials turn out to be
mere symmetric functions:
<p>
k<sup> (n) </sup>_p(j) = \sum<sub> k=0 </sub><sup> p </sup> \ {j \choose k}{{n-j}\choose {p-k}}
<p>
Thus Krawtchouk polynomials may be interpreted as symmetric functions in ....
<p>
K<sup> (n) </sup>_p(i,j) = \sum \prod<sub> k=1 </sub><sup> p </sup> \  s<sub> i_k </sub>
<p>
where sum is taken over all choices of p indices out of n.
</dl>
